\f1\fs22 In the privacy of Sarah\'92s bedroom, her sense of dignity was tested as Kathy performed an invasive, if brief, medical procedure to take a tissue sample from Sarah\'92s uterine lodger.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Just a quick jab, Sarah,\'92 Kathy said with all the bedside manner of a stand-up comedian.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As Sarah reassembled her comportment, Kathy jiggled the sample in a tube of saline, then slotted the tube into a port in her DNA sequencer. Kathy pushed a button and an icon on it glowed blue. The machine hummed like it was grinding peppercorns and glass as it checked, re-checked and checked the data again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How long?\'92 Sarah asked as she sat on the edge of the bed.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91About ten minutes,\'92 Kathy said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah took to her feet, paced to the windows, then the bathroom door, then back to the bed. She almost sat down again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The DNA sequencer was one of those machines designed to go 
\f3\i bing! 
\f1\i0 So it did\'97after about ten hours, it seemed to Sarah.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy pulled her tablet from her satchel, tapped and swiped.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What now?\'92 Sarah quizzed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m comparing the foetal sample with the blood sample I took from Tony just now.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Just one \'85\'92 Kathy gestured on her tablet screen. \'91\'85 second. Ah! Well, what do you know?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92 Sarah\'92s impatience was close to boiling her kettle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It seems Tony is a knight in shining overalls after all. He\'92s not the father.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Are you sure?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I doubt he\'92s even shared a toothbrush with the father, the match is so low.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah went as blank as writer\'92s block. \'91Then \'85 how \'85 who?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Last time, you talked about some weirdos you met at the Vegas Show?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Before the Vegas Show, yes, at the 
\f3\i Memphis Mafia Bar
\f1\i0 .\'92 Sarah went to her hand bag, the only one in her possession now, orphaned from all its sibling handbags lying neglected in her wardrobe back home. Home \'85 She fossicked about in the bag, retrieved a business card, and handed it to Kathy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91
\f3\i Seven Sages 
\f1\i0 Corporation,\'92 Kathy read. \'91Hmmm \'85\'92 She worked her tablet. \'91Ah, here we are.\'92 She scrolled up and down. \'91These guys wanted to get you pregnant?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That was their proposition,\'92 Sarah said. \'92They seemed quite affronted when I rejected them. Although they bought us drinks \'85\'92 Sarah\'92s expression expanded like a helium balloon. \'91They bought us drinks! They drugged us. Those taxers!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy read as she scrolled. \'91Kleoboulos Windus.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That\'92s one,\'92 Sarah confirmed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Pittacus d\'92Lesbos?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Hmmm, don\'92t recall that one \'85 probably one of the ones on hiatus.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Solon Athena?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Solo, yes! And and a Chilo too.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Chilon \cf0 Damagetus\cf2 ,\'92 Kathy read.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes!\'92 Sarah said with increasing hope. \'91Oh, he\'92s a nasty one, that.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91OK, let\'92s if they have DNA records \'85 and compare \'85\'92 Kathy\'92s tablet made one of those chimes that sounded like she\'92d won a small prize in a large lottery. \'91Wow. That was fast. Just one \'85\'92 Kathy gestured on her tablet screen. \'91\'85 second. Ah!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91None of them are the father.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A deflated Sarah collapsed on the bed. \'91So I\'92m screwed.\'92 She buried her face in a pillow.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Not yet. I\'92ll start comparing the foetal sample with all the data swamps.\'92 She tapped and long-pressed. \'91This might take a while.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, I\'92ve got a while. My Elvian Guard isn\'92t letting me go anywhere.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy had a thought. \'91While we\'92re waiting for that, there\'92s another assumption we should check.\'92 After a little more work on her tablet, it chimed again. Kathy scrunched and contorted her face with such committed animation, for a moment Sarah thought she was performing some bizarre gestures for an app on her tablet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, what do you know?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Nothing! I know nothing.\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Let me, um \'85 run it again.\'92 Solemnly, Kathy peeled away the patch adhered to her shoulder. The she rapped her fingers on the sequencer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Is there something wrong?\'92 Sarah persisted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Wrong \'85 no? Science never lies, it just surprises sometimes \'85\'92 Kathy showed Sarah the hand, silencing her next question. \'91This is really weird\'97I mean 
\f3\i really
\f1\i0  weird.\'92 Kathy could not hold back an hysterical laugh. Then with incongruous gravity, she attracted attracted Sarah\'92s gaze and held it. \'91You are not the mother.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A look of confusion and shock took Sarah. \'91So those taxing sages, implanted someone else\'92s embryo in me?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy\'92s jaw tightened and her bottom lip thinned. \'91Well, it appears someone did.\'92 Kathy put her tablet down and hugged Sarah. \'91I\'92m sorry.\'92 She stroked Sarah\'92s hair. \'91At least now you know there\'92s no reason to keep it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah felt empty, despite certain parts of her anatomy being fuller than they had any right to be. \'91Tell me everything you can about this thing inside me.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy released Sarah and reclaimed her tablet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah, uncharacteristically meek, stood and opened the door. Tony nearly fell into her arms.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What\'92s the news?\'92 Tony asked, recovering his balance.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You\'92re not the father.\'92 To Tony\'92s ears, Sarah almost sounded disappointed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Then \'85?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We don\'92t know who yet. Look, Tony, this could take some time.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I don\'92t know what to say.\'92 Tony wanted to take her in his arms, in his burrito-sticky fingers, hold her close, assure her everything would be all right, and then sit her in a comfy chair with a nice cup of tea. He was muddled and kind of grossed out by warm and fuzzy feelings. \'91What will you do?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shook her, resigned. \'91I \'85  I need some rest.\'92 Without meeting Tony\'92s gaze, she gently closed the door.\
\pard\pardeftab360\sl360\slmult1\partightenfactor0

