\f1\fs22 A chime rang and woke Sarah. This was becoming a habit: Kathy and her falling asleep while they waiting for ominous news. This time Kathy was spooning her and cupping her breast. Sarah assumed Kathy\'92s system was still awash with something, probably MDMA, which was making her cuddly but, in any case, Sarah found Kathy\'92s guileless affection comforting.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy roused and stretched, adjusted her bed hair and reached for her tablet. She squinted at what she read. She scrolled and scrolled and scrolled. \'91There are many low-percentage matches to your houseguest,\'92 she told Sarah, who had sat up and was grimacing against dizziness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What does that mean?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Either your houseguest had a great many children \'85 well, no \'85 not possible.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Then?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Then the father is an ancestor of all these,\'92 Kathy said. \'91And by a great many generations. I\'92m sorry, I\'92ve been looking in the wrong data swamps. No wait. Constance, bless her diligence, looks like she saw my search running and figured it out. I\'92ve got quite a few messages from her.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah lay alongside Kathy and peered at her tablet, not that she comprehended much, other than Kathy opened the most recent message, and read.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ah, this is a further analysis of the DNA \'85 
\f3\i I am looking at the helix and it is perfect
\f1\i0 , Constance writes.
\f3\i  I mean absolutely zero copying errors. The sequence is flawless.
\f1\i0  There are usually a one or two,\'92 Kathy explains to Sarah.  \'91Hmmm \'85 
\f3\i viral editing on the growing embryo
\f1\i0  \'85\'92 Beside Kathy, Sarah sat as stiff as a taxidermied trout.
\f3\i  \'91\'85 correcting any errors \'85 optimal development in-utero \'85 a blend of artificial DNA with the base genetic material of the clone
\f1\i0 . 
\f3\i  
\f1\i0 What the tax? Clone?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah wore a mask of horror. \'91What did those sage creeps do to me? What am I, their science experiment?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m sorry.\'92 Kathy held Sarah\'92s hand. \'91You have been violated far worse than any mere assault.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah started sobbing. Kathy cradled her. \'91Hey, we\'92ll figure it out, all right? I said I\'92ll protect you, and I mean it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah wondered if that meant Kathy would drug everyone who might be a threat to her. \'91C\'92mon,\'92 Sarah said and locked her teeth. \'91Tell me whose clone I\'92m carrying.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy nodded, closed Constance\'92s last message and opened the penultimate one. \'91Ah, here we are \'85 someone called Clinton Richard Dawkins, born 26 March 1941, Nairobi. Says here, he was an 
\f3\i evolutionary biologist
\f1\i0  \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What\'92s that?\'92 Sarah asked, pretty sure Kathy wouldn\'92t think less of her for not knowing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Beats me,\'92 Kathy said. \'91Says here, he wrote a number of controversial books, spent most of his life arguing against religion.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah snorted. \'91Good luck with that. The Church of Latter Day Capitalists controls the power in Ulyssia.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yeah, get this: he claimed teaching religion in schools was a form of child abuse.\'92 Kathy thought on it. \'91Seems legit to me.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That scared Sarah a little. Was Kathy prone to activism? Surely someone as smart as her would see questioning the natural order would only lead to a complete breakdown of society.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy thought a bit more. \'91All his books were banned in the Great Enlightenment of the early-to-mid 21
\fs14 \super st
\fs22 \nosupersub  century. Why would these sages want a clone of this guy?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91
\f3\i The prophecy requires a child is born
\f1\i0 ,\'92 Sarah quoted. \'91That\'92s what Chilo said.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Those guys need better pharmaceuticals,\'92 Kathy said. \'91Speaking of which \'85\'92 She fossicked two patches out of her bag and offered Sarah one. Sarah shook her head and managed to look embarrassed at the same time. Kathy shrugged and tore one open for herself. \'91Did they tell you what this great prophecy is?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Chilo\'97the creepy one\'97said they\'92re bringing about the end of the universe.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ah, the first step to creating a successful religion is telling everyone they\'92re going to die\'97worse than they already think they will.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah managed a brief exhausted laugh. \'91I asked them what I should call their Antichrist.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Assuming he\'92s not a Pro-christ.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I think given these books this Dawkins wrote, he\'92s probably anti everything.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Anyway, you know what they said I should call him?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Richard.\'92
